From: Liam Beale <94578031+MrLBeale@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 15:46:03 +1300
Subject: [PATCH] Optionally publish a slice of BAR4 as PCI peer-to-peer memory
 (Blackhole)

Add p2pmem.c: when the p2pmem_devices module parameter names a Blackhole
device, call pci_p2pdma_add_resource() on a configurable slice of BAR4
(p2pmem_offset_mb, p2pmem_size_mb; default one 4 GiB TLB window) so that
the slice gets struct pages and Linux 6.2+ userspace can mmap
/sys/bus/pci/devices/<bdf>/p2pmem/allocate and O_DIRECT-read from an NVMe
drive straight into the card, with no bounce through host RAM.

Off by default (p2pmem_devices is empty). BAR0 is never offered. The driver
programs no TLB window; the user of the memory owns and configures the
window and must keep it (a dma-buf export of the window pins it past
FREE_TLB and close). A read-only "p2pmem" sysfs group reports the slice and
the TLB windows that cover it.

RESET_DEVICE with a destructive flag returns EBUSY while any of the slice is
allocated, as it already does while a TLB window is exported as a dma-buf,
and holds the whole slice for the length of the reset so that nothing can
start a DMA into it meanwhile. System suspend is refused while any of the
slice is allocated. Removing a device with the slice allocated logs a
warning: the PCI core offers no way to revoke the mappings sooner.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01WPkg1xnoRyTfVHFMzui62d
