+
+## P2P Memory Attributes (optional)
+
+When the `p2pmem_devices` module parameter names a Blackhole device, a slice of
+its BAR4 is published as PCI peer-to-peer memory (see the module parameters
+`p2pmem_devices`, `p2pmem_offset_mb`, `p2pmem_size_mb` and `p2pmem_publish`).
+The memory is then allocated from userspace through
+`/sys/bus/pci/devices/<YYYY:XX:ZZ.Z>/p2pmem/allocate` (Linux 6.2 or later).
+
+The driver programs no TLB window: whoever uses the memory must own a TLB
+window that covers it (`ALLOCATE_TLB` and `CONFIGURE_TLB`) for as long as the
+memory is mapped.
+
+**Location**: `/sys/class/tenstorrent/tenstorrent!<N>/p2pmem/`, present only
+when the memory was published. All files are readable by root only.
+
+| sysfs Filename | Description                                                         |
+|----------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|
+| `bar`          | The BAR the slice is in (always 4)                                  |
+| `offset`       | Byte offset of the slice inside the BAR                             |
+| `size`         | Slice length in bytes                                               |
+| `bus_addr`     | PCI bus address of the first byte of the slice (hex)                |
+| `tlb_first`    | First inbound TLB window id overlapping the slice (-1 if none)      |
+| `tlb_last`     | Last inbound TLB window id overlapping the slice (-1 if none)       |
+
+Whether any of the slice is allocated is the PCI core's `p2pmem/size` and
+`p2pmem/available` under `/sys/bus/pci/devices/<YYYY:XX:ZZ.Z>/` (equal when
+nothing is). While any of it is allocated, `RESET_DEVICE` with a destructive
+flag, and system suspend, return `EBUSY`: a reset under in-flight peer-to-peer
+DMA can wedge the host. During a reset the whole slice is held, so
+`p2pmem/allocate` fails with `ENOMEM`.
